\DOC LOCAL_SUB_P384_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_SUB_P384_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Steps an {eventually}-style machine-code goal through an inlined
modular-subtraction subroutine, advancing the symbolic state to the next state
index and recording the subroutine's arithmetic specification as a new
assumption.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} selecting which of the available
instantiations/offset variants of the subtraction block to apply; the second is
a {string list} naming the destination and the two source operands, in that
order, used both to build the result assumption and to locate the corresponding
memory/register locations.
{
   A ?- eventually ... s_i
   ======================================  LOCAL_SUB_P384_TAC n [dest; src1; src2]
   A' ?- eventually ... s_j
}
\noindent Here {j} is a later state index, and the conclusion differs only in
that final state term. In passing from {A} to {A'}, every fact mentioning the
old state — the program-counter value, {bytes_loaded}, the register/memory
reads, and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation — is removed and replaced by
the corresponding fact about the new state, with the {MAYCHANGE} component
possibly extended by the newly written location; reads whose locations were
overwritten are dropped, and a read of the destination location yielding the
named result is added. One further assumption is added: an implication stating
that if the two source values are below the modulus then the (integer)
destination value equals the difference of the sources reduced modulo the
prime. Some applications also re-add, unchanged, a block of previously present
arithmetic side conditions.

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show this single kind of transformation, on goals of this
{eventually} form carrying a large assumption list of machine-state facts.

\ENDDOC
